    "compose": "# Postgres without a published port: reach it from another container on the\n# compose network, or through `docker compose exec`. Publishing 5432 on the host\n# is the most common way a dev database becomes a production incident.\nname: arvion-postgres\n\nservices:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres:16.4-alpine\n    restart: unless-stopped\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_DB: ${POSTGRES_DB:-app}\n      POSTGRES_USER: ${POSTGRES_USER:-app}\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-change-me}\n      POSTGRES_INITDB_ARGS: \"--auth-host=scram-sha-256\"\n    volumes:\n      - postgres-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U $${POSTGRES_USER} -d $${POSTGRES_DB}\"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n      start_period: 20s\n    mem_limit: 512m\n    cpus: 1.0\n    networks:\n      - database\n\nvolumes:\n  postgres-data:\n\nnetworks:\n  database:\n    driver: bridge\n",
    "envExample": "# Copy to .env and change the password before first start.\nPOSTGRES_DB=app\nPOSTGRES_USER=app\nPOSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me\n",
    "readme": "# Postgres\n\nPostgres 16 on a private compose network. No host port is published, so the\ndatabase is reachable only from containers on the `database` network.\n\n## Run\n\n```bash\ncp .env.example .env      # then change POSTGRES_PASSWORD\ndocker compose up -d\ndocker compose exec postgres psql -U app -d app\n```\n\n## Notes\n\n- The healthcheck uses `pg_isready` with the actual user and database, so a\n  server that is up but not accepting connections still reads as unhealthy.\n- `POSTGRES_INITDB_ARGS` forces SCRAM authentication for host connections.\n- Data lives in the named `postgres-data` volume. `docker compose down -v`\n  deletes it; that is deliberate.\n- Add `ports: [\"127.0.0.1:5432:5432\"]` only if you need a GUI client on the host,\n  and never bind it to `0.0.0.0`.\n",
